function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:r}})}var t={id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp`,title:`The spoken web`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`We're growing more used to chatting to our computers, phones and smart speakers through voice assistants like Amazon's Alexa, Apple's Siri and Microsoft's Cortana. Blind and partially sighted people have been using text-to-speech converters for decades.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Out of these assistants, Siri is the most well-known. The assistant uses voice inquiries and a natural-language user interface (界面) to answer questions. The software adapts to users' individual language usages, searches, and preferences, with continuing use.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Some think voice could soon take over from typing and clicking as the main way to interact online. But what are the challenges of moving to "the spoken web"?`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`What use is written online content if you can't read? That is the situation facing illiterate (不识字的) African farmers. They are often denied crucial information the web offers many others. With a literacy rate in some parts of Africa at only 22.6%, farmers are often "underpaid for their produce because they might be unaware of the prevailing prices," says Francis Dittoh, a researcher behind Mr. Meteo, a speech-based weather information service.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`"The most frequently heard complaint is about rainfall predictions," says Mr. Dittoh, who lives in Tamale, northern Ghana. "They tell us the methods their forefathers used to predict the weather don't seem to work as well these days." This is down to climate change, he believes. Yet knowing when it's going to rain is vital for farmers wanting to sow seeds, irrigate crops or take their animals out to the fields to feed on grass.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Mr. Dittoh says the idea of converting online weather reports into speech came from the farmers themselves, after a workshop in the village of Guabuligah. "They came up with this," he says. Mr. Meteo takes the online weather forecast, converts it to a short recording in the appropriate language and makes it available on a basic phone. Farmers ring up to receive the information. The local language Dagbani is spoken by 1.2 million people but is not served by any online translation applications. The service was designed to be cheap and easy to run, says Mr. Dittoh. He plans to begin field tests this month, working with Tamale's Savanna Agricultural Research Institute.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`The spoken web could also help the one-in-five adults in Europe and the U.S. with poor reading skills. But building the spoken web—web-to-voice and voice-to-web—isn't straightforward. For software to understand pizza is served at Italian restaurants is easy. To cover multiple domains and to be able to have a conversation with users on every single topic is still a long way off.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`So although many computer assistants can answer simple questions about the weather and play music for us, anything resembling a wide-ranging human conversation is decades away. Artificial intelligence just isn't smart enough yet. Even turning your voice into text—automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest problems to solve, as there are as many ways to pronounce things as there are people on the planet.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Siri has often been praised for its ability to interpret our casual language and deliver very specific and accurate results, sometimes even providing additional information. But it is still somewhat restricted, particularly when the language moves away from stiffer commands into more human interactions. In one example, the phrase "Send a text to Jason, Clint, Sam, and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant" was interpreted as sending a message to Jason only, containing the text "Clint Sam and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant". It has also been noted that Siri lacks a proper editing function, as saying "Edit message to say: we're at Silver Cloud restaurant and you should come find us" generates "Clint Sam and Lee saying we're having dinner at Silver Cloud restaurant to say we're at Silver Cloud restaurant and you should come find us".`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`Using voice interaction feels far more intimate than surfing the net the old-fashioned way. This is intentional as the informal tone of the assistant helps create an emotional attachment. But if something speaks, it must also listen. Our phones are always near us and they are collecting data about us all the time. This has already raised privacy concerns. The American Civil Liberties Union has stated that digital assistants create a threat to privacy from hackers. Some people have other concerns. They worry assistants will one day be used to deliver advertising directly to us.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`But digital voices need more personality to make them popular. Robots are not yet witty, Siri is boring. The benefits of using voice instead of tapping fingers obviously depend on the context. Doctors completing online forms about their patients by speech, for example, can dictate 150 words a minute, three times faster than typing on a keyboard. This enables them to spend less time on administration and more time with patients.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Last year, speech recognition company Nuance helped a doctors' surgery in Dukinfield, near Manchester, set up a speech system for the practice's six doctors. Now they can dictate notes on a patient's health condition and treatment and a smart assistant automatically enters the information into the right fields on a web form. Previously, the doctors made voice recordings that were then transcribed by secretaries -- a process that was costly and likely to cause delays. The new system means letters to patients now have more detail.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`Using voice also makes sense when you're doing other things with your hands. Think about when you're cooking, and you just want to know what the next step in the recipe is. Your hands are covered with oil; you're not going to get on the iPad, so it's a lot more natural to talk. And speech obviously makes sense when you're driving. In the U.S., 29% of drivers admit they surf behind the wheel, according to insurance firm State Farm. This is up from 13% in 2009. No wonder using mobile phones while driving causes more crashes a year than drunk driving, says the U.S. National Safety Council.`}]},n={"cet4-2023-06-s1":{subtitle:`2023 年 6 月 · 第 1 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2023年6月英语四级第1套真题，写作考就网课优缺点调查作回应并提出改进建议，翻译考中国终身教育/继续教育，听力给出原文并由站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: Suppose your university is conducting a survey to collect students' opinions of online classes. You are to write a response to the survey about their advantages and disadvantages, and what improvements can be made. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Online classes have become increasingly prevalent in higher education, and students have mixed feelings about them. On one hand, they offer unparalleled flexibility: students can learn at their own pace and from any location with an internet connection. Recorded lectures also allow us to review difficult material when necessary. However, the lack of face-to-face interaction can leave students feeling isolated and make it difficult to seek immediate help. Some students also lack suitable devices or reliable internet access, which prevents them from participating fully. To improve online classes, universities should strengthen their technology infrastructure and offer equipment loans to students who need them. Teachers could organize regular live discussions and group projects to encourage participation. Online tutoring would provide further academic support. These improvements could preserve the convenience of online learning while making it more accessible and engaging.`,guidance:[`第1段：一句话引出现象＋亮出态度。"Online classes have become increasingly prevalent… and students have mixed feelings about them" 既交代网课在高校普及，又点出学生感受不一，为下文优缺点对比铺路，不急于单边表态。`,`第2段前半：On one hand 摆优点。unparalleled flexibility、learn at their own pace、from any location、recorded lectures allow us to review——从灵活自主、地点自由、录播可回放三方面写足优势，回应 survey 的 advantages。`,`第2段后半：However 摆缺点。lack of face-to-face interaction → feeling isolated、难以立即求助；some students lack suitable devices or reliable internet access → 无法充分参与。优缺点形成对照，呼应首段 mixed feelings，回应 disadvantages。`,`第3段：分层给改进建议并收束。universities should strengthen technology infrastructure and offer equipment loans；Teachers could organize regular live discussions and group projects；Online tutoring would provide further academic support——学校、教师、辅导三层建议分别对应设施、互动、学业支持；末句 These improvements could preserve the convenience… while making it more accessible and engaging 回扣优点与改进。三段结构，词数 120–180。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Suppose your university is conducting a survey to collect students' opinions of online classes. You are to write a response to the survey about their advantages and disadvantages, and what improvements can be made. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`应用文（对学校调查的 response 回应）写作，三个任务点缺一不可：网课优点、缺点、改进建议。开篇用 Online classes have become increasingly prevalent… and students have mixed feelings 引出话题；主体段以 On one hand… However… 对比优缺点（灵活、可回放 vs 缺乏面对面互动、设备与网络门槛），再用 To improve… / Teachers could… / Online tutoring would… 给出具体改进，语气客观、有建设性；末段概括改进效果收尾。三段结构，词数 120–180。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear three news reports. At the end of each news report, you will hear two or three questions. Both the news report and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`A woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding wild animals. The 55-year-old woman's neighbours blame her for making the area's rat problem worse. Newly installed cameras captured several rats active in the middle of the day. Neighbours say that it's even worse during the night. The woman and her parents, who own the home, told reporters that she's being charged for feeding birds. "Who would have known just loving animals gets you that much trouble," she said. It is prohibited to feed any wild animals, including birds, in Rhode Island City. Nevertheless, while the woman's intention was to feed birds, it is clear that rats were also benefiting. Antony Moretti, Director of the City Administration, said he saw more than 20 rats near the woman's home. He said it will take months to get the problem under control.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`To prepare for eventually sending astronauts to Mars, NASA began taking applications Friday for four people to live for a year in Mars Dune Alpha. That's a 1,700-square-foot Martian habitat inside a building in Houston. The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars. They will have limited communications with family, restricted food and resources. NASA is planning three experiments with the first one starting in the fall next year. Food will all be ready to eat space food. Some plants will be grown, but not potatoes like in the movie The Martian. "We want to understand how humans perform in them," said Lead Scientist Grace Douglas. "We're looking at Mars' realistic situations." The application process opened Friday and they're not seeking just anybody. The requirements are strict, including a master's degree in a science, engineering or math field or pilot experience. Only American citizens or permanent U.S. residents are acceptable. Applicants must be between 30 and 55 and in good physical health. Attitude is key, said former Canadian astronaut Chris Hadfield. He said the participants need to be super competent, resourceful and not rely on other people to feel comfortable.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`A Japanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player. Nagoya Mayor Takashi Kawamura had praised athlete Mio Goto during a public media event. He asked her to put the medal around his neck. Kawamura then bit into it. Biting a medal in front of journalists and photographers has become a common pose for Olympic medalists. However, it is only for the winners themselves, not others. "I'm really sorry that I hurt the treasure of the gold medalist," Kawamura told reporters Thursday. The mayor said the medal was undamaged, though he offered to pay for the cost of a new one. Goto, however, has accepted the International Olympic Committee's offer of a replacement, according to Japanese media reports. The scene broadcast on television prompted thousands of complaints to City Hall. Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures and that it was disrespectful and unacceptable for Kawamura to bite one. "I would cry if that happened to me," said another athlete, Naohisa Takato. "I handle my own gold medal so gently that I would not scratch it."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`M: What's that orange thing on your computer screen? W: It's something I bought yesterday. It's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light. I have one that I use to cover my phone screen too. M: What do you mean by blue light? W: Blue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural. For example, from computers, phones, televisions, and other electronic devices. M: So blue light is harmful and that's why you want to block it? W: It isn't that simple. Blue light isn't necessarily bad for us. In fact, we need blue light during the day to be healthy. But too much blue light, especially from electronic devices, can harm our health by weakening our vision and making it harder for us to fall asleep. And poor sleep can cause all sorts of health problems. M: I'm not so sure that sleep is nearly as important as people always say it is. I haven't slept enough in months because I have too much work to do. And I feel fine. And it's the same for most of my friends. Poor sleep might be a problem for older people, but surely young people can handle late nights. W: Well, the research I've read shows that sleep is probably even more important than we thought, and that not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems, like obesity and heart disease. And all the artificial blue light from electronic devices means we have to try harder to sleep well. M: Maybe you're right. I'm on my computer very late most nights, and that's probably why I don't sleep enough.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`M: As a kid, did you know what job you wanted to do when you grew up? W: No, I didn't. And I got sick every time adults asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up. M: It's the same with me, and I'm tired of people asking that question of my 10-year-old daughter. My daughter's stock answers are basketball player, pop singer, mechanical engineer. W: Adults love that last one, as it's the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious. When she was much younger, my daughter used to say she wanted to be queen of the clouds, which I loved. That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children, springing from their boundless imaginations. M: Yes, we grown-ups can be tedious and limiting in our need for reality, and we teach a very gloomy image of adulthood, that whatever our children's future holds, it must be seen within the context of a job. How utterly overwhelming and dull. When people ask my son what he wants to be when he grows up, I have to swallow the urge to say, hey, back off my kid's dreams. W: We can't dismiss the idea that teenagers have to plan to do something after they finish school, and parents are entitled to hope it's more than simply spending 10 hours a day playing computer games. M: But asking, what do you want to be? isn't going to lead a child to a fulfilled life, rather it leads to false expectations and a high chance of disappointment. W: Exactly. We should be helping our kids understand who they are, even if that means letting go of who we think they should be.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Greater internet access correlates directly with improved health care, education, and economic development. People living in rural areas, however, lag behind in online use, which limits their access to government services, banking, and job opportunities. Nowhere is this challenge clearer than in Africa. Most Africans live in rural areas that are tough to wire for internet access. Now, some phone companies are trying to introduce internet-ready phones into African markets. Certain companies have started selling simple smartphones for only $20. Previously, the lowest price had been around $40, well out of reach for many people. These devices are powered by software from the giant electronics company KaiOS Technologies Ltd. Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable, but KaiOS went the other way. It made every effort to keep the essential capabilities of smartphones, but strip out costs and preserve battery life for people who likely have inadequate access to electricity. The KaiOS devices offer an alternative to the more expensive models that remain out of reach of many Africans and contribute to the digital divide. The body of KaiOS phones is as basic as it gets. Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad. They're designed for 3G networks because 4G coverage doesn't reach two-thirds of Africa's customers. In total, KaiOS phones are made from about $15 worth of parts, while Apple's top-of-the-line iPhone has $390 worth of stuff.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`For years, using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap. By contrast, fossil fuel plastic was more expensive. Thus, the sustainable option was an economic option too. But now, it is cheaper for major manufacturers to use new plastic. According to one recent business report, recycled plastic now costs an extra $72 a ton compared with newly made plastic. This may be because of consumer demands. They are pushing for more recycled plastics in new products. Meanwhile, new plastic is becoming cheaper. This is because of a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S. The price increase of recycled plastic could cost sustainable manufacturers an extra $250 million a year. Smaller manufacturers may also be forced to use new plastic to reduce costs. Makers of clear plastic bottles may also opt for new fossil fuel-based plastic to save money. Plastic packaging makers are being pressured to use more recycled plastic. This is done in hopes of reducing the enormous amount of plastic pollution in the oceans. The U.K. government plans to tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in their products. Additionally, the government is planning to increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market. This could mean incentives for new recycling plants. Additionally, recycling facilities may be improved at a local council level and recycled plastic could be imported. This would help increase the amount of recycled plastic in circulation.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan, naps are for sale. A 45-minute session in a darkened enclosure with peace and quiet costs $25. To be clear, this institution is no hotel. This is a nap joint. It sells the idea of the nap as much as the nap itself. Is a nap worth $25? The answer is obviously yes. Here, at this point in the argument, it's traditional for me to bring up all the studies that show the benefits of napping. But do you really need experts to tell you that? Just look at the world around you at 2.30 in the afternoon. I've been working from home for more than 10 years now and the quality and quantity of work I can do emerges directly from my ability to concentrate. I don't understand how people have creative careers without napping. Every day at about 1 p.m., everyone faces the same choice. Sleep until 2 p.m. and then work until 5, or daydream and drift around social media and attend pointless meetings until 7 p.m. The friends I have who still work in offices inform me their bosses insist that they take the second option and that napping is associated with laziness. I genuinely find it odd, for if you nap properly, it's like waking up from a full night's sleep and you can double your day's worth of concentration.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-1`,stem:`What do we learn from the report about the 55-year-old woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She was involved in a conflict with bird lovers.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She was charged with mistreating animals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She was on bad terms with her neighbors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She was accused of violating a city law.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "a woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding wild animals"，这名 55 岁女子被指控违反罗得岛城市禁止喂野生动物的法律，即被控违反城市法规，选 D；B) mistreating animals（虐待动物）与原文 feeding（喂食）不符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-1`,stem:`What did the Director of the City Administration say at the end of the news report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will take time to solve the rat problem.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) All wild animals should be well protected.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The woman was not to blame for the situation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) No one should go unpunished for violating law.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "it will take months to get the problem under control"，市政主管说要把鼠患控制住需要几个月，即解决问题需要时间，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-2`,stem:`What does NASA require the paid volunteers to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Communicate with astronauts in Mars Dune Alpha.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Work in an environment resembling Mars.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Build a Martian habitat in Houston.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Send in applications before Friday.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars"，受薪志愿者将在与火星相似的环境中工作，选 B；火星栖息地是休斯敦已有的建筑，并非让志愿者去建造，C 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-2`,stem:`What will the participants in the project eat?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ready-made food.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Food that is organic.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Food they grow.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Potatoes mostly.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Food will all be ready to eat space food"，食物全部是即食太空食品，选 A；虽会种一些植物，但明确 "not potatoes like in the movie The Martian"，C、D 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`,stem:`What does the news report say about the Japanese Mayor Takashi Kawamura?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He apologized for scratching an athlete's gold medal.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He was asked to present a gold medal to Miu Goto.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He bit a softball player's Olympic gold medal.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He attracted public attention at a media event.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "A Japanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player"，名古屋市长咬了垒球运动员的奥运金牌并为此道歉，选 C；A) scratching（刮花）与原文 the medal was undamaged 不符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`,stem:`What did Mayor Takashi Kawamura offer to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Have another picture taken with the Olympic medalist.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Apologize to the International Olympic Committee.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Get the damaged medal repaired.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay for the cost of a new medal.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "he offered to pay for the cost of a new one"，市长主动提出支付一枚新金牌的费用，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`,stem:`What did some Olympians say they would do with their medals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Allow no one to touch them.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) See them as symbols of honor.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Treat them as treasures.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Keep them in a safe place.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures"，一些奥运选手说把奖牌视为珍宝，选 C；末句 "I handle my own gold medal so gently that I would not scratch it" 进一步印证。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`,stem:`What did the woman do to her computer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She covered its screen with a plastic sheet.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She connected it with her smartphone.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She decorated it with colorful stickers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She bought some new software for it.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文女士说买了 "a plastic sheet that blocks blue light" 贴在电脑屏幕上，也有一张贴在手机屏幕上，即在屏幕上覆盖防蓝光塑料膜，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`,stem:`What does the woman say about blue light?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may not be simply blue.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It includes unnatural light.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is more harmful to young people.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It induces people to fall asleep.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Blue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural"，蓝光既含自然光，也含来自电脑、手机等非自然光源的光，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`,stem:`Why does the man say he hasn't slept enough for months?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He has had much trouble falling asleep.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He has had some sort of health problems.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He has stayed up playing computer games.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He has been burdened with excessive work.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "I haven't slept enough in months because I have too much work to do"，男士说几个月睡眠不足是因为工作太多、负担过重，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`,stem:`What has the woman learned from the research she has read?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Exposure to blue light is the chief cause of obesity.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sleep may be more important than people assumed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Sleep may also be negatively affected by natural light.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Overuse of electronic devices may cause heart disease.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "sleep is probably even more important than we thought"，女士读到的研究表明睡眠可能比人们原先以为的更重要，选 B；肥胖、心脏病是睡眠不足可能导致的健康问题，蓝光并非其 chief cause，A、D 偷换因果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`,stem:`What question were both speakers fed up with when they were kids?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) What they wanted to be when grown up.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) What their favorite cartoon character was.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) What they learned from computer games.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) What they liked to do most after school.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`对话开头女士说 "I got sick every time adults asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up"，男士回应 "It's the same with me"，两人都厌倦被问长大后想做什么，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`,stem:`What occupation do adults see as both sensible and ambitious, according to the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A stock broker.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A pop singer.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A mechanical engineer.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A basketball player.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "My daughter's stock answers are basketball player, pop singer, mechanical engineer" 后女士说 "Adults love that last one, as it's the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious"，last one 指机械工程师，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`,stem:`What kind of goal setting does the woman like to see in children?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ambitious.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sensible.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Imaginative.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Practical.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children, springing from their boundless imaginations"，女士赞许孩子 "想当云朵女王" 这类源自无尽想象的目标设定，即富有想象力，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman suggest adults should do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Relax their strict control of their kids.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Help their kids understand themselves.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Impose their own dreams on their kids.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Dismiss their high expectations of their kids.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`对话末句 "We should be helping our kids understand who they are, even if that means letting go of who we think they should be"，女士建议帮助孩子认识自己、放下成人预设的期待，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`,stem:`What are a number of phone companies trying to do in Africa?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Set up company branches.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Improve its infrastructure.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Introduce iPhones into its markets.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Promote Internet-ready phones.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "some phone companies are trying to introduce internet-ready phones into African markets"，一些手机公司正试图把可上网手机引入非洲市场，选 D；文中提到 20 美元的简易智能机，并非苹果 iPhone，C 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`,stem:`How do KaiOS smartphones differ from smartphones of most other companies?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They cater to Africans' needs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are more expensive models.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are more powerful and capable.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They boast the longest battery life.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable, but KaiOS went the other way… keep the essential capabilities… strip out costs and preserve battery life"，KaiOS 反其道而行，保留基本功能、削减成本、保住续航，正是迎合非洲用户的使用条件与需求，选 A；D) the longest battery life 属无根据的最高级。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`,stem:`What are KaiOS smartphones equipped with?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A large touchscreen.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) An old-school keypad.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A voice-response device.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A digitally-designed system.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad"，KaiOS 手机没有触摸屏，而用老式键盘操控，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`,stem:`What is said about using recycled plastic to make plastic products in the past?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It ensured sustainable economic growth.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was strongly opposed by manufacturers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was cheaper than using fossil fuel plastic.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It satisfied consumer demands on the whole.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "For years, using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap. By contrast, fossil fuel plastic was more expensive"，过去再生塑料比化石燃料塑料便宜，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`,stem:`What has led to a more competitive price for new plastic?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The capacity to mass produce it.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The U.S. federal government's regulations.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A boom in market demand for clear plastic bottles.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A rapid increase in U.S. petroleum chemical production.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "new plastic is becoming cheaper. This is because of a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S."，美国石化生产激增使新塑料价格更具竞争力，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`,stem:`What does the U.K. government plan to do about plastic?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Require companies to use 30% of new plastic.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Increase the supply of new plastic in the market.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Reduce the amount of plastic pollution in local areas.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Take measures to promote the use of recycled plastic.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文英国政府计划 "tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in their products"，并 "increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market"，即对不用足 30% 再生塑料的公司征税并增加再生塑料供应，整体是促进再生塑料的使用，选 D；A、B 把 recycled 错写成 new plastic。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`,stem:`What do we learn about the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It studies dreams.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It rents a place for nap-takers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is a hotel for business people.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is a nap research institute.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan, naps are for sale. A 45-minute session in a darkened enclosure… costs $25"，这家店按 45 分钟出租昏暗安静的打盹空间，明确 "this institution is no hotel"，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`,stem:`Why does the speaker ask us to look at the world around us at 2.30 in the afternoon?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To find out creative people's work performance.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To see how many people can go without napping.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To understand the obvious importance of napping.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To feel how difficult it is to get his idea across.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`作者先反问 "do you really need experts to tell you that? Just look at the world around you at 2.30 in the afternoon"，意在说明午睡的重要性显而易见、无需专家论证，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`,stem:`What do we learn about the quantity and quality of the speaker's work?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They decline due to pointless meetings.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They depend on his ability to concentrate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They enable him to enjoy a creative career.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are affected by the overuse of social media.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "the quality and quantity of work I can do emerges directly from my ability to concentrate"，工作质与量直接取决于专注能力，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`,stem:`What does the speaker say he finds odd?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Some bosses associate napping with laziness.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many office workers nap during work hours.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Some bosses can concentrate without napping.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many of his friends daydream in the office.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "I genuinely find it odd… their bosses insist that they take the second option and that napping is associated with laziness"，作者觉得老板们坚持不午睡、把午睡和懒惰挂钩这件事很奇怪，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`abstracts`,B:`abundant`,C:`awareness`,D:`conscience`,E:`exact`,F:`increasingly`,G:`intense`,H:`mend`,I:`nearly`,J:`reckoning`,K:`reducing`,L:`sew`,M:`shrink`,N:`statistics`,O:`textile`},passages:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-1`,text:`You probably haven't taken the time to think of all the work that went into creating the shirt on your back. I mean, how hard it could be to create fabric and [26] it into a shirt shape. Don't machines do all that? Well, creating fabric from cotton, which is the most [27] clothing material, is actually a process that involves a lot of water, 2,700 liters per shirt to be [28]. Take a look at the video below from National Geographic for some more mind-blowing [29] about cotton clothing production.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-2`,text:`Clean water is [30] becoming one of the most sought-after resources in the world. Given how large the [31] and cotton industries are, they take up a lot of our fresh water demands across the world, according to The Huffington Post. The video from National Geographic was created to spread [32] of how environmentally harmful cotton is. But the situation can be made better. Through better water management and farming practices, water usage in cotton production can be cut down by [33] 40 percent.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-3`,text:`Called "Better Cotton", this environmentally conscious product will save millions of liters of water a year simply from [34] the demands of cotton production. Cotton doesn't have to go, since it is, after all, one of the most useful cash crops across the globe. However, as water supplies [35], farmers and consumers need to be more conscious of the effect that these products have on the environment as a whole.`}]},t,{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-1`,text:`The United States is facing a housing crisis: Affordable housing is inadequate, while luxury homes abound (充裕), and homelessness remains a persistent problem. Despite this, popular culture and the housing industry market happiness as living with both more space and more amenities (便利设施). Big houses are advertized as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-2`,text:`This is reflected in our homes. The average single-family home built in the United States before 1970 was less than 1,500 square feet in size. By 2016, the average size of a new, single-family home was 2,422 square feet. What's more, homes built in the 2000s were more likely than earlier models to have more of all types of spaces; bedrooms, bathrooms, living rooms, dining rooms, recreation rooms and garages.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-3`,text:`There are consequences of living big. As middle-class houses have grown larger, two things have happened. First, large houses take time to maintain, so cleaners and other low-wage service workers are required to keep these houses in order. Second, once-public spaces, where people from diverse backgrounds used to come together, have increasingly become privatized, leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many. Take swimming pools. While in 1950, only 2,500 U.S. families owned pools, by 1999 this number was 4 million. At the same time, public municipal pools were often closed, leaving low-income people nowhere to swim.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-4`,text:`The trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions. Should Americans accept a system in which the middle and upper classes enjoy a luxurious lifestyle, using the low-wage labor of others? Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-5`,text:`I believe neither is acceptable. We must change the way we think: living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces. A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.`}]},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-1`,text:`Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition. Ambitious people strongly desire accomplishments and are willing to take more risks and spend more effort to get them.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-2`,text:`Overall, this is a positive quality, especially for people trying to build their own businesses. Apparently, if you're more naturally driven to set goals, you are more likely to succeed.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-3`,text:`Actually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-4`,text:`One major side effect of excessive ambition is the tendency to focus too determinedly on one particular vision or end goal. This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur. If a new competitor emerges to threaten your business, you may need to change direction, even if that means straying from your original vision. If you have too much ambition, you'll find this hard, if not impossible.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-5`,text:`Few people are successful when they try to build their first brand. Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-6`,text:`It's a clear departure from the intended plan toward the intended goal. For people with limited ambition, however, failure is viewed as something closer to reality. Remember, failure is inevitable, and every failure you survive is a learning experience.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-7`,text:`Ambitious people tend to be more materialistically successful than their non-ambitious counterparts. However, they're only slightly happier than their less-ambitious counterparts, and tend to live significantly shorter lives. This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional "success," such success means nothing for their health and happiness-and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-8`,text:`Clearly, some amount of ambition is good for your motivation. Without any ambition, you wouldn't start your own business, set or achieve goals and get far in life. But an excess of ambition can also be dangerous, putting you at risk of burnout, stubbornness and even a shorter life.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`…how hard it could be to create fabric and __26__ it into a shirt shape.`,answer:`L`,explanation:`sew 缝制：把布料"缝"成衬衫形状，create fabric and sew it into a shirt shape 语义连贯；and 连接并列的动词原形，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-1`,quote:`how hard it could be to create fabric and [26] it into a shirt shape.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`Well, creating fabric from cotton, which is the most __27__ clothing material, is actually a process that involves a lot of water…`,answer:`B`,explanation:`abundant 丰富的、充裕的：棉花是"最丰富常见的"服装原料，the most abundant clothing material 合情合理；the most 后需形容词，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-1`,quote:`creating fabric from cotton, which is the most [27] clothing material`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`…a process that involves a lot of water, 2,700 liters per shirt to be __28__.`,answer:`E`,explanation:`exact 确切的：每件衬衫耗水 2700 升，to be exact 为固定插入语，表示"确切地说"，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-1`,quote:`2,700 liters per shirt to be [28].`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`Take a look at the video below from National Geographic for some more mind-blowing __29__ about cotton clothing production.`,answer:`N`,explanation:`statistics 数据、统计数字：视频提供更多关于棉服生产、令人震惊的"数据"；some more 后接名词复数，且语义上 mind-blowing statistics 搭配自然，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-1`,quote:`for some more mind-blowing [29] about cotton clothing production.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`Clean water is __30__ becoming one of the most sought-after resources in the world.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`increasingly 日益、越来越：清洁水"正日益成为"世界上炙手可热的资源之一；修饰 becoming 需副词，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-2`,quote:`Clean water is [30] becoming one of the most sought-after resources in the world.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`Given how large the __31__ and cotton industries are, they take up a lot of our fresh water demands across the world…`,answer:`O`,explanation:`textile 纺织的：the textile and cotton industries 指"纺织和棉花产业"，规模巨大、耗费大量淡水；与 cotton industries 并列作定语，选 O。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-2`,quote:`Given how large the [31] and cotton industries are`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`The video from National Geographic was created to spread __32__ of how environmentally harmful cotton is.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`awareness 意识：视频旨在传播棉花对环境危害的"意识"，spread awareness of 为常见搭配；spread 后需名词，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-2`,quote:`created to spread [32] of how environmentally harmful cotton is.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`Through better water management and farming practices, water usage in cotton production can be cut down by __33__ 40 percent.`,answer:`I`,explanation:`nearly 将近、几乎：通过改进用水管理与耕作方式，棉花生产用水量可减少"近"40%；修饰数字 40 percent 需副词，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-2`,quote:`water usage in cotton production can be cut down by [33] 40 percent.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`Called "Better Cotton", this environmentally conscious product will save millions of liters of water a year simply from __34__ the demands of cotton production.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`reducing 减少：这一环保产品仅通过"减少"棉花生产需求每年就能节省数百万升水；介词 from 后接动名词，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-3`,quote:`simply from [34] the demands of cotton production.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`However, as water supplies __35__, farmers and consumers need to be more conscious of the effect that these products have on the environment as a whole.`,answer:`M`,explanation:`shrink 缩减、减少：随着水源"缩减"，农民与消费者须更留意这些产品对环境的整体影响；as 引导时间状语从句，主语 supplies 后需动词原形，选 M。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-cp-3`,quote:`However, as water supplies [35], farmers and consumers need to be more conscious`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`According to Francis Dittoh, their speech-based weather information service was meant to be inexpensive and easy to use.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "The service was designed to be cheap and easy to run, says Mr. Dittoh"——Dittoh 说该语音天气服务的设计初衷就是便宜、易运行，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Using voice instead of typing enables doctors to spend more time taking care of patients.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`K 段 "Doctors… can dictate 150 words a minute, three times faster than typing on a keyboard. This enables them to spend less time on administration and more time with patients"——语音输入让医生少花时间做文书、多花时间陪病人，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-K`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`It is extremely difficult to convert voice into text because of different pronunciations.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`H 段 "Even turning your voice into text—automatic speech recognition—is one of the hardest problems to solve, as there are as many ways to pronounce things as there are people"——发音方式因人而异，语音转文字极难，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-H`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`African farmers unable to read often don't have access to important information conveyed online.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "What use is written online content if you can't read? That is the situation facing illiterate (不识字的) African farmers. They are often denied crucial information the web offers many others"——不识字的非洲农民被剥夺网上重要信息，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Some phone users worry advertisers will take advantage of voice assistants to send ads directly to them.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "They worry assistants will one day be used to deliver advertising directly to us"——有人担心语音助手将来被用来直接推送广告，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`The spoken web is helpful when one's hands are occupied.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`M 段 "Using voice also makes sense when you're doing other things with your hands… Your hands are covered with oil… so it's a lot more natural to talk"——手被占用（做饭、开车）时语音更方便，选 M。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-M`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Some people believe online interaction would soon depend mainly on voice.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`C 段 "Some think voice could soon take over from typing and clicking as the main way to interact online"——有人认为语音很快会取代打字点击成为在线互动的主要方式，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-C`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`Setting up a spoken web is by no means an easy task.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`G 段 "But building the spoken web—web-to-voice and voice-to-web—isn't straightforward… To cover multiple domains… is still a long way off"——搭建口语网络绝非易事，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-G`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Weather information is extremely important to farmers.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "Yet knowing when it's going to rain is vital for farmers wanting to sow seeds, irrigate crops or take their animals out to the fields"——知道何时下雨对农民播种、灌溉、放牧至关重要，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Some people are concerned about privacy because their phones are constantly collecting their personal information.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "Our phones are always near us and they are collecting data about us all the time. This has already raised privacy concerns"——手机时刻在收集用户信息，引发隐私担忧，选 J（与第 40 题同段，信息匹配允许一段多用）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What are big houses promoted to be in the United States?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A luxury for the homeless.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A reward for industriousness.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) An abundant source of comforts.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) An absolute necessity for happiness.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第一段 "Big houses are advertized as a reward for hard work and diligence"——大房子被宣传为勤奋努力的回报，industriousness 即 hard work and diligence，选 B；D) 与原文 "turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury" 相悖。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-1`,quote:`Big houses are advertized as a reward for hard work and diligence, turning housing from a basic necessity into a luxury.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the consequences of living big?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Many Americans' quality of life has become lower.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) People from diverse backgrounds no longer socialize.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) People no longer have access to public swimming pools.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many Americans' private life has been negatively affected.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第三段指出，大房子使公共资源日益私有化，"a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many"——许多人的生活质量下降，选 A；C) "再也无法使用公共泳池"过于绝对（原文是公共泳池常被关闭）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-3`,quote:`leading to a reduction in the number of public facilities available to all, and a reduced quality of life for many.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What questions arise from living big?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Questions related to moral principles.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Questions having to do with labor cost.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Questions about what lifestyle to promote.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Questions concerning housing development.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第四段 "The trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions"——住房越来越大带来"伦理问题"，ethical 即 moral principles，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-4`,quote:`The trend for bigger housing thus poses ethical questions.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What kind of social system does the author think is unacceptable?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) One in which the wealthy exploit the low-wage laborers building their houses.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) One in which the rich purchase amenities at an increasingly unjustifiable price.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) One in which the upper classes deprive the lower classes of affordable housing.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) One in which the affluent enjoy a more comfortable life at the expense of the poor.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第四段反问 "an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor"，第五段 "I believe neither is acceptable"——作者认为富人以牺牲穷人为代价享受更多便利的制度不可接受，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-4`,quote:`Are we willing to accept a system in which an increase in amenities purchased by the affluent means a reduction in amenities for the poor?`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author advocate for people to live well?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Finding ways to turn private spaces into public ones.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Building more houses affordable to those less affluent.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) More public spaces created for everyone to enjoy.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) All amenities made accessible to the rich and the poor alike.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`末段 "living well does not need to mean having more private spaces; instead, it could mean having more public spaces… create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all"——作者主张为大家创造更多公共空间，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p1-5`,quote:`A better goal than building bigger houses for some is to create more publicly accessible spaces and amenities for all.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author think of most entrepreneurs?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are more willing to risk their own lives.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are more ambitious than ordinary people.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They achieve greater nonconventional success.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have more positive qualities than most of us.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第一段 "Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition"——创业群体的抱负高于平均水平，即比普通人更有野心，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-1`,quote:`Most of us in the entrepreneurial community are blessed—or cursed—with higher-than-average ambition.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply by saying "this isn't always the case" (Line 1, Para. 3)?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ambitious people may not have a greater chance of success.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Ambitious people may not have more positive qualities.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Entrepreneurs' ambition does as much good as harm.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Entrepreneurs are more naturally driven to success.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第二段称有目标导向者更易成功，第三段却转折 "Actually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good"——暗示有抱负者未必有更大的成功几率，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-3`,quote:`Actually, this isn't always the case. In fact, in some cases, extreme ambition may end up doing more harm than good.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say is of extreme importance for one to become a successful entrepreneur?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Holding on to one's original vision.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Being able to adapt to new situations.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Focusing determinedly on one particular goal.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Avoiding radical change in one's career direction.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第四段 "it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur"——适应新环境的能力对成功创业至关重要，选 B；A、C 恰是原文批评的"执着于单一愿景"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-4`,quote:`This is problematic because it hinders your ability to adapt to new circumstances, which is vital if you want to be a successful entrepreneur.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`How do the most ambitious entrepreneurs regard failure in their endeavor?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will awaken them to reality.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is a lesson they have to learn.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It means the end of their career.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will result in a slow recovery.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第五段 "for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from"——野心最大的创业者把失败看作灾难性、无法从中恢复的，即等同于职业生涯终结，选 C；B 是原文所说的"野心有限者"的看法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-5`,quote:`Unfortunately, for the most ambitious entrepreneurs, a failure is seen as disastrous, and impossible to recover from.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author advise us to do concerning ambition?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Distinguish between conventional success and our life goal.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Follow the example of the most ambitious entrepreneurs.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Avoid taking unnecessary risks when starting a business.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Prioritize health and happiness over material success.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`倒数第二段 "they're only slightly happier… and tend to live significantly shorter lives… such success means nothing for their health and happiness"——作者暗示物质上的传统成功不及健康与幸福重要，故应把健康幸福置于物质成功之上，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s1-p2-7`,quote:`This implies that even though ambitious people are more likely to achieve conventional "success," such success means nothing for their health and happiness-and if you don't have health and happiness, what else could possibly matter?`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`China is increasingly focusing on lifelong education, and the development of continuing education is an effective way to build a lifelong education system. As the base for talent cultivation, universities possess advanced teaching philosophies and superior teaching resources, and should naturally become the primary providers of continuing education. Therefore, in recent years, many universities have adapted to social demands, strengthened communication with employers, and endeavored to explore a new path for the development of continuing education that fits China's national conditions, so that continuing education can play a greater role in the country's development strategy.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s1-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`中国越来越重视终身教育,发展继续教育是构建终身教育体系的有效途径。高校作为人才培养的基地,拥有先进的教学理念和优越的教学资源,理应成为继续教育的办学主体。因此,近年来许多高校适应社会需求,加强与用人单位沟通，努力探索一条符合中国国情的继续教育发展新路,以使继续教育在国家发展战略中发挥更大的作用。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"终身教育"lifelong education、"继续教育"continuing education 为全文核心词，首句用 China is increasingly focusing on… and the development of… is an effective way to build… 承接。②"高校作为人才培养的基地"译作 As the base for talent cultivation, universities…；"拥有先进的教学理念和优越的教学资源"用 possess advanced teaching philosophies and superior teaching resources；"理应成为继续教育的办学主体"用 should naturally become the primary providers of continuing education。③"适应社会需求、加强与用人单位沟通、努力探索…新路"三个动词并列，译作 have adapted to…, strengthened…, and endeavored to explore…；"符合中国国情的"处理为定语从句 that fits China's national conditions。④末句"以使…发挥更大的作用"用 so that continuing education can play a greater role in the country's development strategy 表目的；全文以现在时 / 现在完成时为主，注意中文短句的合并与逻辑衔接。`,score:15}]}]}};export{n as default};